From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 14:14:53 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: implement modify_ldt for x86 tasks

x86 tasks of the x86-64 userspace slice get an LDT, which modify_ldt()
reads and writes as an x86-64 kernel does: function 0 reads the
entries in use, zero-filled to the byte count; 2 gives 128 zero bytes;
1 and 0x11 write one entry in the old and the new mode, with Linux's
checks and fill_ldt()'s descriptor; anything else is ENOSYS, and the
result is an int, zero-extended in RAX. A fork child gets a copy, and
execve starts without one. Wine's i386 NtSetLdtEntries() is a user.

This kernel refuses 16-bit segments, as an x86-64 kernel built
without CONFIG_X86_16BIT does: the Darwin host runs no 16-bit code.

The host's engines read the table while the mm's tasks run (bridge
ABI 20): every guest entry hands its address over in the guest
registers' fp. The table holds all 8192 descriptors from the first
write on, a write changes one descriptor with a single 8-byte store,
and the table is freed only with the mm. An empty slot answers every
lookup as an index past Linux's LDT limit does, so the host needs no
entry count.

The breakpoint event's #NP, which a selector load of a segment that is
not present raises, reaches the task as SIGBUS with SI_KERNEL and the
selector in the error code, as on x86 Linux.
